<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lezgins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lezgin people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lezgins are a Caucasian people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, living mainly in the Samur valley and the neighbouring mountains. They speak Lezgian, a Northeast Caucasian language, and are mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims. In Georgian and Persian sources the name Lezgin was often applied loosely to all the mountaineers of Dagestan who raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lezgins.jpg" alt="Lezgins" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zurgh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lezgian_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Lezgin is very old. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> gives the Greek form Légai and the Latin Legae, with parallels in Armenian, Georgian, Arabic and Persian. Greek historians including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Strabo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> mention a people called Legoi in Caucasian Albania, and Arab writers of the 9th and 10th centuries mention a kingdom called Lakz in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> the word Lezgin was applied to all the ethnic groups of Dagestan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tribes speaking Lezgic languages formed a union of 26 tribes within the state of Caucasian Albania in the 4th century BCE, and the state was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in 513 BCE. Rule passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> to the Sassanid Persians in 252-253. The Lezgins were probably first introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as early as the 8th century but remained primarily animist until the 15th, when Muslim influence grew. In the early 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> consolidated control over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>.</p><p>In 1721 the Lezgin leader and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> cleric Haji Davud sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, the provincial capital of Shirvan, an event that contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>. He founded a short-lived Shirvan Khanate recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Russia gained southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> in 1813 and consolidated its control by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> in 1828. Many Lezgins fought beside the Avar Imam Shamil in the Great Caucasian War, until his defeat in 1859.</p><p>In 1918 the Lezgin population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Kuba</a> district resisted the forced establishment of Soviet rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and their Dashnak allies. A punitive detachment burned 162 villages, 27 of them completely, and more than 16,000 civilians were killed in the district, among them 14,000 Lezgins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Lezgin society is structured around djamaat, unions of clans, within which there are about 200 to 300 clans called syhils. Each syhil has its own village quarter and mountain, and a gathering of the male residents, the Kim, addresses important questions of village life. The society has traditionally been egalitarian. An unwritten code of honour, Lezgiwal, covers every sphere of life and implies moral behaviour, generosity and the will to safeguard the honour of women. The eagle, lek in Lezgian, is a common symbol and is linked to the value of freedom.</p><p>Lezgin belongs to the Lezgic branch of the Northeast Caucasian family and has three mutually intelligible dialects: Kurin, Akhti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Kuba</a>. Some Lezgins celebrate the pre-Islamic festival Yaran Suvar. In the mountains they traditionally practised sheep and goat husbandry and crafts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lezgins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sunni <b>Lezgins</b> led by Haji Davud Bey took Shamakhi, the capital of the Safavid province of Shirvan, after the central government abandoned its counter-campaign at a critical moment.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population was mixed, including Azerbaijani, Persian, Tat-speaking Jewish and <b>Lezgin</b> communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/derbent-khanate">Derbent Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers balanced between the Safavid Empire, the khanates of Shaki and Shirvan, the <b>Lezgin</b> free communities of Jar and Belokan and later Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/elisu-sultanate">Elisu Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Kakheti endured Persian governors and <b>Lezgin</b> raids.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kingdom of Kakheti</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1812 the Russian authorities formed the Kurin Khanate from <b>Lezgin</b> communities in the Samur river area of southern Dagestan, which had been free societies.</blockquote><a href="/state/kurin-khanate">Kurin Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Lakz is known chiefly from Arab writers of the 10th century such as al-Masudi, who describe a numerous and warlike people in the mountains west of Derbent, generally identified with the ancestors of the <b>Lezgins</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakz">Lakz</a></li></ul></section>
